        'Instructions for the "lab-focus" skill: <skill name="lab-focus" status="running"> Source:…',
      text: 'Instructions for the "lab-focus" skill:\n\n<skill name="lab-focus" status="running">\nSource: /Users/miguelgarglez/.config/devin/skills/lab-focus/SKILL.md\nBase directory: /Users/miguelgarglez/.config/devin/skills/lab-focus\n\n# lab-focus\n\nThis is the Lab method for one product Miguel chooses, with Miguel in the chat. It reuses `~/.config/devin/skills/lab-build/SKILL.md` and its `references/` (`taste.md` and `infra.md`). Read all three first. The differences from an unattended Lab run are listed here, and everything else in lab-build applies as written.\n\n## What changes\n\n- **No scouting and no picking.** Skip Steps 1 and 2. The product is the argument: an idea in Miguel\'s words, or the slug of an existing repo under `~/Developer`. Still apply the Step 2 ambition gate to the idea. If the idea fails it, say why and propose the smallest change that would make it pass. Do not refuse.\n- **Miguel is here.** You may ask him questions, but only questions that experiment cannot settle (taste, scope, naming, budget). Show him the 2-3 directions from Step 4 as screenshots and let him choose. Show him each milestone as it lands. His word overrides a reviewer\'s score, and you record it in `decisions.tsv`.\n- **Claim the unit anyway.** Lab lanes may be running. Before you start, run `lab-claim take unit:<slug>` with `LAB_RUN_ID=focus-<slug>` and `LAB_RUN_PID` set to this shell\'s PID, through `lab-state` for `units.tsv`, exactly as Step 3 says. If a lane already owns the unit, stop and tell Miguel.\n- **Time is not boxed to runs.** Milestones still end in verified states, and the Handoff section in the brief still gets written whenever you stop. Another session, focused or a Lab lane, can then continue.\n- **Same gates.** The design gate, `lab-preship`, the 7-day pin rule, the three-engine checks, the viewport check, and the budgets in `references/infra.md` all apply unchanged. `main` moves only after `lab-preship` passes.\n- **Same outputs.** The output is a public repo, a live URL, a HyperFrames launch video, a README, a Typefully draft (never published), an announcement through `lab-state announce`, retrospective moments, and a knowledge base note through `kb-sync.sh`.\n\n## Starting an existing product\n\nIf the argument is an existing repo, read its brief in `~/Developer/_lab/briefs/<slug>.md` and its latest Handoff, if they exist. Then ask Miguel what he wants changed, write it as a `redo.md`-style entry in the brief, and follow lab-build Step 0 item 4 (redo scope rules). A bug gets a fix and not a redesign, and the product keeps its identity.\n\n## Traceability: every session, every agent\n\nWhen Miguel needs the AI sessions as a deliverable (for a hackathon or a review), every agent must stay traceable:\n\n- Run every agent with its working directory inside the project: Devin itself, Codex (`lab-codex -C <project>`, plus `-c model_reasoning_summary=detailed` so its reasoning is readable), and Cursor CLI (launched from the project directory). Subagents are fine, because they live inside the Devin session.\n- At the end, run `lab-sessions-export <project> <out>/sesiones`. It collects every Devin session (including subagent branches, which the official ATIF export leaves out), every Codex rollout from both accounts, and every Cursor chat, verbatim, with a `manifest.json` in start order. Also write the official ATIF export of the main Devin session with `devin -r <id> --export <out>/sesiones/devin-atif-<id>.json`.\n- Read the exporter\'s secret scan before anything leaves the machine, and tell Miguel what it found. The exports stay verbatim, so the fix for a leaked secret is to rotate it, never to edit the export.\n</skill>',
