      title:
        "I'm designing the dispatch.ts data flow for intent handling, planning to use the intent's…",
      text: "I'm designing the dispatch.ts data flow for intent handling, planning to use the intent's own id as the stable bank_reference so repeated attempts stay consistent rather than generating a fresh identifier each time.\n\nI also need to add an 'unknown' status to cover ambiguous bank responses: a successful POST or a replay marks the intent completed, a 4xx client error marks it definitively failed, but a 5xx or timeout requires checking the bank's operation lookup endpoint -- if the operation exists it's completed, if not it's safe to retry.\n\nRunning through the simulated bank scenarios confirms this logic: the early-503 case safely retries with the same reference, the read-unavailable case ends in 'unknown' for later reconciliation, and the slow-response case resolves to completed once the lookup catches up with the delayed commit. Now I need to update dispatchTransfer so the catch block properly routes to 'unknown' instead of defaulting to failed.\n\nI'm defining a DispatchResult type that distinguishes completed operations from unknown ones carrying a reference and error, throwing only for truly definitive failures. I also want the 'unknown' status surfaced honestly to the client — adding a message field to the tool result, and in submitTransfer setting a clear pending-operation notice in the error field so the UI displays it properly. Finally I'm sketching out a reconcileIntent function to handle follow-up resolution of these unknown-status intents.\n\nI'll start the simulator server and set BANK_URL before importing config, then write a new transfers test file and update the test script to cover it. I'm mapping out the key scenarios: lost-response and reject-before should each produce exactly one completed operation, slow-response needs to tolerate the timeout/lookup delay, and read-unavailable should leave the intent in 'unknown' status with the bank_reference stored, then converge to completed on a retried call with the same intentId to confirm idempotency.\n\nI'm also considering intermittent failures across three distinct intents (all should settle to exactly one operation each and complete), insufficient funds (should fail cleanly with no operation created), and double-submission of an already-completed intent — deciding it's fine to let the bank replay rather than short-circuiting locally, since that stays honest and idempotent. For the HTTP-level test, the simulator server needs to seed bank accounts if none exist and start listening before tests run against it.\n\nSince I can't modify the simulator to export the server instance for cleanup, I'll spawn it as a child process with the right environment variables instead, matching how dev.ts launches it, and kill it in the test's `after` hook to avoid hanging the test runner.\n\nFor version control, since no git repo exists yet, I'll have the sidekick initialize one locally (excluding .git from the final zip), committing the baseline first, then the failing test, then the fix—giving a clean traceable history.\n\nNow I'll write the brief covering the architecture, types, flow, and this git/test-cleanup strategy.\n\n",
