      title:
        'The retest confirms the fix: 4 failures before became 19 passes after, since the retry lo…',
      text: "The retest confirms the fix: 4 failures before became 19 passes after, since the retry logic no longer lets a second request slip through with a fresh ref. I'm approving P0.1 — the diff is minimal and lines up with the design, aside from a minor nit about the unknown-transfer event not capturing useful data yet, which P1 will address. Moving on to P0.2, I'm now sketching the confirmation flow and starting to define the approvals data shape.\n\nI'm placing the validation logic inside authorizeTransfer itself, keeping it as the single gate for money movement, with the route handler staying thin. For the self-transfer edge case, I'll let the bank's 400 rejection handle it rather than duplicating that check.\n\nFor the agent side, I'm adding a `confirm_transfer({approvalId})` tool that calls transferMoney with the stored intentId and approvalId once the customer says \"yes\" — treating that chat confirmation as the explicit holder approval the contract requires, while noting the prompt-injection risk is mitigated since the approval only exists after a legitimate prior request.\n\nTo prevent the model from confirming itself within the same turn, I'm designing a structural guard: since intents store a run_id, confirm_transfer should require that intent.run_id differs from the current run's id, forcing confirmation to happen in a later message. I'll update the UI panel and run.ts instructions so both the chat and dashboard confirmation paths route through this same gate.\n\nI'm also thinking through expiry handling — proposals expire after 10 minutes, and confirming an expired one should return a failed ActionResult with a message asking for a new proposal, rather than throwing an HTTP error, since this needs to surface cleanly through the tool interface.\n\nI'm also working out the consumed_at timing: it should be marked before dispatch so that if dispatch returns an unknown outcome, the approval is already consumed and can't be retried through the same confirm endpoint. For unknown dispatch results, I'm leaning toward relying on the reconciliation path rather than allowing a retry with the same intentId and approvalId.\n\nNow I'm checking the UI side — `submitTransfer` in page.tsx already surfaces a `requires_confirmation` notice with the approvals panel for confirming, so I want to verify the panel correctly renders the proposal's payload fields and that the manual Transfers tab also routes through the confirmation flow rather than bypassing it.\n\nFor testing, I'm planning cases around `transferMoney`: initiating without an approvalId should yield 'requires_confirmation' with zero operations and a pending approval row, confirming with a valid approvalId should complete the transfer and mark consumed_at, and confirming a second time should fail since the approval is already used even though the bank might replay the request idempotently.\n\nI need to cover tampered payloads, expired approvals, same-run confirmation attempts, and cross-user confirmation attempts, all rejecting with 0 operations. The existing P0.1 tests need updating to a two-step flow, so I'll add a `transferConfirmed` helper that proposes then confirms; HTTP-level verification can be done via curl rather than a dedicated route test.\n\nThen I want to verify this through an actual live chat exchange — asking to send money, confirming no funds move until the user says \"yes\", then checking it executes exactly once — saving that as evidence. I also need to check the approvals panel in page.tsx around those specific lines and align the tool definitions and instructions, then simplify the confirm route to just load the approval and call transferMoney with the intent and approval ids, letting authorizeTransfer handle validation.\n\nP0.1 aprobado: diff mínimo, 4 tests fallaban antes y 19 pasan después, y los cuatro escenarios del banco verificados por API. Ahora paso a P0.2 (confirmación), revisando el panel de propuestas de la UI para encajar el diseño con lo existente.\n\n",
