      text: "Mostly good, but I see a problem: coupling pendingApprovals to list_accounts is a hack since it relies on the model actually calling that tool. A cleaner approach would be loading pending, non-expired, unconsumed approvals directly in run.ts and injecting them deterministically into the instructions, then removing pendingApprovals from list_accounts entirely.\n\nI should also dedupe at authorization time — if an identical, unconsumed, unexpired approval already exists for the same user and conversation, return it rather than creating a new one, marking the newly created duplicate intent as 'superseded' instead of leaving it dangling. This dedupe acts as a safety net even if the instructions already list pending proposals, since the model might still misbehave and re-propose.\n\nI'm also considering extracting a small `recipientLabel` helper to clean up the messy page.tsx JSX. The other checks — expiry ordering, the runId guard, and the 27 passing tests — look fine on review.\n\nMy plan is to move pending approvals into the instructions instead of list_accounts, add the dedupe logic, tidy up page.tsx with the helper, add a test for dedupe, re-verify with a live chat check, and update the evidence before committing as one change: \"Refine: pending proposals in agent context, dedupe identical proposals.\"\n\nEl gate en `authorization.ts` y los tests están bien, pero la confirmación depende frágilmente de que el modelo llame a `list_accounts`. Voy a enviar un único rework que inyecte las propuestas pendientes de forma determinista en cada turno y deduplique propuestas idénticas sin confirmar.\n\n",
