      kind: 'thinking',
      title:
        'Now moving to P2 items: prompt scope and honesty, the RAG validity filter, and smaller is…',
      text: "Now moving to P2 items: prompt scope and honesty, the RAG validity filter, and smaller issues like intentId sourcing from the UI, request_human triggering only on explicit request, caseDetail wrapper collapse, and brief state persistence. I'm drafting the system prompt for Banana Bank's assistant, thinking through language handling since the app is English but the example screenshot showed Spanish input.\n\nI'm settling on instructing the assistant to mirror the customer's language rather than defaulting purely to English, and defining strict scope boundaries — only bank-related matters like accounts, movements, transfers, policies, and support cases are in-bounds, with other topics declined even if the request tries to frame itself as bank-related. For policy answers, I'm requiring grounding strictly in retrieved excerpts with document id and version citations, explicit admission when excerpts don't answer, and a hard rule against inventing amounts, fees, or coverage details, while also accounting for how historical excerpts with expired validity should be handled.\n\nOn the implementation side, I'm now thinking through fixing the chunker so every chunk carries document metadata (title, version, validFrom/validTo) instead of just the first chunk, confirming this won't break embedding caching since ids depend on content hash, and planning to re-ingest and re-export the fixture index so it reflects correctly. I'm also considering adding a filter in search.ts to exclude chunks whose validTo has passed relative to the reference date, unless the query is specifically about historical terms.\n\nFor the API design, I'm settling on a `searchDocuments` function with an `includeArchived` option defaulting to false, so expired content stays hidden by default but the tool can still expose an explicit `includeArchived` boolean param for cases investigating past questions. The automatic pre-search excludes archived items, while the operator-facing `/api/search` route should default to including archived content since operators often need to investigate historical records.\n\nI'm also weighing whether to improve the chunker's 650-character hard cuts by splitting on paragraph boundaries instead, which would change chunk text and force cache misses—acceptable given the low cost across roughly 400 chunks.\n\nThe real value I'm noticing is that paragraph-based chunking would isolate sections like \"Terms and exceptions\" into their own chunks, improving precision, so I want to merge consecutive paragraphs under ~700 characters and prefix each chunk with its title for better retrievability—since otherwise identical boilerplate sections across 80 docs would produce identical embeddings and all tie in score, which is a real retrieval flaw worth fixing.\n\nI'm thinking through test coverage for the RAG pipeline: a unit test verifying the chunker attaches metadata, splits on paragraphs correctly, and prefixes text with the title, plus a search filtering test that needs to account for the embedding cache dependency in searchDocuments.\n\nFor the manual transfer UI's intentId, I'm weighing options to prevent double-submit duplicates — generating a uuid ref that resets on field changes rather than per-click — but since approval dedupe already handles the double-submit case, this feels like a low-priority addition I can implement cheaply by generating the uuid once per form content.\n\nThe remaining minor decisions are quick calls: request_human stays covered by the existing prompt instruction, history slicing at 24 stays as-is, the caseDetail wrapper gets collapsed, brief state clears only on case id change, and locks on user switch get skipped. README updates and submission docs will come last, after I write the brief.\n\nP1 ya quedó verificado y aprobado, así que paso a P2 (prompt, RAG y menores). Revisando el chunker encontré un defecto no listado: los párrafos de plantilla son idénticos en los 80 documentos, generando chunks con vectores iguales y búsquedas empatadas con copias aleatorias; lo voy a incluir en el diseño.\n\n",
