        'Codex raised a valid issue: relying on the model to judge "yes in chat" as consent is inh…',
      text: "Codex raised a valid issue: relying on the model to judge \"yes in chat\" as consent is inherently fuzzy for a chat agent. I'm weighing alternatives — requiring UI-button-only confirmation (safer but worse UX) versus a structural check where the server verifies the customer's latest message literally contains an affirmative token like \"yes\" or \"confirm.\"\n\nA more bank-grade option is binding confirmation to a short code generated from the proposal (like derived from approvalId), where the customer must reply \"confirm <code>\" and the server checks that the triggering user message actually contains that code — making the confirmation cryptographically tied to the specific transfer proposal rather than just an LLM judgment call.\n\nI'll derive the code from the approvalId (6 hex chars, uppercase) and have the prompt ask the customer to reply with that specific code to authorize, allowing lowercase and spacing when matching — this also guards against confirming the wrong proposal when multiple are pending. Separately, I need to handle the case where the bank response body fails to parse as JSON after a successful status: I'll wrap that parse so a failure throws a BankError (502/504 \"Unreadable bank response\") instead of an unclassified error, so dispatch treats it as ambiguous rather than a hard failure.\n\nFor testing that path, I'll spin up a tiny fake HTTP server on a separate test port that returns malformed JSON with a 200 status, setting BANK_URL before import so client.test.ts can hit it directly.\n\nI also need to fix a race where a brief response tagged to a previous case bleeds into the UI after the selected case changes — I'll tag responses with the case id and discard stale ones. And there's a bug where resubmitting an identical manual form after confirmation re-proposes against an already-completed intent; I'll reset manualIntentId in the UI after confirm completes, and also guard server-side against re-authorizing a completed intent.\n\nFor the no-approval transfer branch, instead of failing when an intent is already completed, I want transferMoney to detect a prior 'completed' status, look up the existing operation via the bank's GET /v1/operations/:ref endpoint, and return a replay result with the intentId and operationId so retries are idempotent and honest rather than erroring. If status is 'unknown' it should continue to the normal dispatch path. I also need to make sure the expiration logic doesn't overwrite an intent's status if an operation already exists for it.\n\nFor approval expiry, I'm now reasoning that expiry checks should only apply when the intent is in 'awaiting_confirmation' status -- if the intent is 'unknown' with a consumed approval, I should allow confirmation regardless of expiry since re-dispatch just replays the open money question, leaving reconciliation to the operator for truly unknown cases.\n\nOn deduplication merging distinct intents, I'm weighing whether my approach of deduping only unconfirmed proposals within the same conversation inside a 10-minute window is an acceptable tradeoff to prevent the model from creating near-duplicate intents.\n\nThinking more about this, with confirmation codes each proposal becomes distinct, so the customer just confirms whichever code they were given, and the pending-proposals list in the instructions already mitigates clutter from re-proposing. This makes me lean toward removing dedupe entirely -- since no money moves on a mere proposal, a harmless duplicate proposal with its own code is an acceptable cost for simpler logic.\n\nFor the race condition in multi-process execution, I'm designing an atomic consume operation: updating the approvals table to mark an approval consumed only if it hasn't been consumed yet, checking the row-change count to detect whether it was already used.\n\nFor the prompt injection concern in copilot, I'm thinking to wrap untrusted customer content in delimiters and explicitly tell the model to treat it as data, not instructions. I'll also validate retrieved sources against known document IDs, fall back gracefully on malformed JSON output, and switch to persisting the full brief in the event log for auditability rather than just counts.\n\nFor the UI, instead of trying to mark briefs as stale after reconciliation, I'll simplify by just clearing the brief entirely when a case is reconciled or closed, reverting my earlier plan.\n\nFor the schema duplication issue, I'll use zod 4's built-in `toJSONSchema` to derive JSON schema directly from the zod schema rather than maintaining both separately, skip the ActionResult typing for now as a bigger task, and the retry-dedupe concern is moot since I removed that logic. I should also wait for the Cursor review before dispatching anything further.\n\n",
