        'Both reviewers are converging on the same core issues: the confirm_transfer flow lacks cu…',
      text: "Both reviewers are converging on the same core issues: the confirm_transfer flow lacks customer consent verification, the \"unknown\" state handling has problems around approval expiry and duplicate intent IDs when retried, and the copilot has gaps in JSON fallback handling, injection risk, and unvalidated sources—plus a concern that the last message in a conversation might actually be the assistant's own.\n\nFor the fix, I want to properly model intents as a state machine (created → awaiting_confirmation → processing → completed/failed/unknown), with a resolver that checks the bank reference: a 200 response confirms completion and logs a resolution event, a 404 means the bank never recorded it so it's safe to mark failed and let the customer retry, and errors leave it unknown. This resolver would run when the dashboard loads, surfacing a \"pending\" panel for intents still being verified.\n\nThe agent should also get unconfirmed operations injected into its instructions so it never proposes a duplicate transfer, and the operation_status tool should auto-resolve matching unknown intents to completed. I also want to attempt resolution at the start of sendMessage before building instructions, and extend the operator reconcile tool's \"fixable\" logic to cover expired, awaiting_confirmation, and superseded states whenever the bank confirms the operation exists.\n\nServer-side, I should add a guard in authorizeTransfer: if a user already has an unresolved identical intent, try resolving it first, and if it's still unknown, block the new proposal with a message pointing to the pending reference rather than creating a duplicate. This respects the rule that a transport error doesn't prove rejection, and expiry logic should only apply to awaiting_confirmation states, not unknown ones.\n\nFor consent binding on confirm_transfer, I'm designing a check where the approval flow generates a short confirmation code from the approvalId, and the server verifies that the current run's user message actually contains that code before honoring the confirmation—tying consent explicitly to the user's own message rather than just the tool call.\n\nNow I need to handle the route path differently from the chat/tool path, since route confirmations come from a UI click with no corresponding user message. I'm adding a `confirmedBy` field ('message' vs 'click') to the tool context so authorization logic knows which validation applies, defaulting to requiring an explicit value rather than silently assuming one, and updating tests to pass this explicitly.\n\nI'm also updating the prompt instructions: the customer must authorize by replying with the exact confirmation code shown in the proposal, not just a plain \"yes\". This makes the run-id same-run guard redundant for the chat path, so I'm removing that guard and its test in favor of code-based verification, while keeping the click path separate. I'm also dropping the payload dedupe logic for proposals since the pending-proposals context now handles that, though I still need to address the edge case where an identical payload arrives while a prior proposal is still unresolved.\n\nFor the manual form, I'm resetting the manual intent id after confirmation and any terminal result, and updating transferMoney so that if the previous intent already completed with a reference, it replays the completed result idempotently via a GET; if the status is unknown, it resolves rather than re-proposing — so the no-approval branch in authorizeTransfer now checks for unknown status first and returns the resolved result. In client.ts, I'm making a failed body parse throw a 502 BankError with an \"unreadable response\" message so it's treated as ambiguous, and I need to add a test for that case.\n\nI'll add a test with a fake server returning invalid JSON to confirm bankRequest throws BankError with status ≥500 on garbage bodies, plus a 504 case for connection refused. For atomicity I'm using conditional updates — consuming approvals only when not already consumed, and assigning bank references only when unset, checking affected row counts and reading back the result. For the Copilot prompt, I'm wrapping untrusted sections with explicit markers and adding instructions clarifying that customer messages and document excerpts are untrusted input.\n\nI'm also defining the retrieval logic to grab the last user message, handling invalid JSON by throwing an HttpError with a clear message, filtering sources to only valid retrieved document IDs, and storing the full brief for auditability. I'm checking whether zod 4's `toJSONSchema` enforces `additionalProperties: false` and required fields correctly by default, or whether that only happens with strict objects or output-mode schemas.\n\nOn the UI side, I'll discard a stale brief if the selected case changes before it arrives, and clear it on reconcile or close since facts may have shifted. I also need to prevent internal-only documents from leaking into draftReply, instructing that such content can only be cited in suggestedActions, and I'll add an audience field to the document entries. For the minor fixes, I'll keep closing a case simple by blocking it with a 409 if there are unresolved fixable reconciliation entries, and leave the random run_id as acceptable.\n\nI'm adding a `code` field to failure reasons in authorization (not_found, mismatch, expired, already_used, code_required) since it's cheap to include. For tests, I need to update approvals tests to cover codes, remove the old dedupe tests and replace them with ones covering: unknown intents blocking identical new proposals, resolving unknown intents into completed states with bank ops (or 404 into failed), expired approvals on unknown intents still resolving, completed intents retried returning the same op id, and updating client.test.ts. This is a large but coherent hardening pass, so I'll write it all out now.\n\nAmbos revisores confirman los mismos fallos críticos: la confirmación por chat depende del criterio del modelo, el estado `unknown` acaba forzando un doble cargo, y el copiloto mezcla texto del cliente con hechos. Voy a consolidar todo en una oleada de endurecimiento con un diseño único antes de continuar.\n\n",
