      kind: 'thinking',
      title:
        "I found a real bug: an expired first attempt sets status to 'expired', but a second attem…",
      text: "I found a real bug: an expired first attempt sets status to 'expired', but a second attempt skips the expiry check entirely since status no longer equals 'awaiting_confirmation', letting it execute anyway. The fix is to only bypass the expiry check when intent status is 'unknown', so expiry still applies for both 'awaiting_confirmation' and 'expired' states.\n\nThere's also a second gap: the duplicate-block check only runs at proposal time, so a pre-existing proposal can bypass it by confirming directly. I need to run that identical-payload check at confirmation time too, blocking if another intent with the same payload is 'unknown' or 'processing' — though stale 'processing' intents (from a crashed dispatch) could then block forever unless resolveIntent is extended to also resolve 'processing' intents with a bank_reference via a status GET.\n\nI'm also reconsidering the negation heuristic for confirmation messages — trying to tighten it so only messages matching an allowlist of confirmation words plus the code are accepted, while rejecting anything containing negation terms like \"no\", \"not\", \"don't\", or \"cancel\" to avoid false-positive confirmations.\n\nFor B3, I'm thinking the operation_status check can just reuse a single fetched operation to mark a pending intent resolved directly, rather than re-querying. For B4, I'm deciding whether copilot.brief events should be excluded or relabeled in the agent activity feed.\n\nFor A5, the fix is resetting manualIntentId on refresh if the corresponding approval is no longer pending, avoiding stale replay of a chat-confirmed proposal. For A6, I'm dedupe-ing repeated proposals for the same intent by returning the existing unconsumed, unexpired approval tied to that intent_id rather than creating a duplicate. Now I'm looking at how custom tool arguments get injected into the agent activity display.\n\n",
