      text: "I'm refining the authorization logic. The original code checked for the confirmation code in an all-caps, no-spaces string. I'm updating it to normalize the user's message to lowercase, replace non-alphanumeric characters with spaces, collapse multiple spaces, and then remove all spaces before checking for the confirmation code. This handles variations like \"ABC-123\" more robustly.\n\nI'm reconsidering how to remove the confirmation code from the normalized message. Simply collapsing the string and removing the code substring breaks word boundaries, making it impossible to re-split into valid words. A better approach is to iterate through the words, checking if a single word or a sequence of consecutive words matches the code. If found, I'll remove those words and then validate the remaining words against an allowed list.\n\nI'm refining the normalization to handle accented characters like 'í' and 'ó' by keeping them in the allowed character class, while still removing other non-alphanumeric characters. The test cases confirm this approach works for various inputs, including those with spaces and mixed casing, correctly identifying valid confirmation codes or rejecting invalid ones based on the remaining words after code extraction.",
