      kind: 'thinking',
      title:
        "I'm designing the confirmation rule to be strict: normalize the message by lowercasing an…",
      text: "I'm designing the confirmation rule to be strict: normalize the message by lowercasing and stripping punctuation, then accept it only if, after removing the code itself, what remains consists solely of whitespace and words from an allowlist of affirmative terms like \"confirm,\" \"yes,\" \"ok,\" \"please,\" and similar variants in other languages.\n\nFor unresolved duplicates, I'm defining equivalence by matching fromAccountId, toAccountId, and amountCents, then checking this both at proposal and confirmation time. I'm extending resolveIntent to handle 'processing' statuses with or without a bank reference, treating 404 responses as still in-flight unless the intent is old enough that a stale crash is likely, in which case I need a mitigation path so these don't block forever.\n\nIn client.ts, I'm making sure the original HTTP status is preserved when the bank response body can't be parsed, so a 404 with an unreadable HTML body still surfaces as a 404 rather than defaulting to something generic.\n\nIn run.ts, I'm adding a block to the prompt listing operations that resolved since the last turn so the model can inform the customer instead of re-proposing them, and I'm fixing the stale \"yes\" confirmation guidance to require the actual confirmation code. I'm also adding dedupe logic so an existing unconsumed, unexpired approval for the same intent is reused, and tightening expiry checks so intents transition to 'expired' correctly while 'unknown' stays untouched. Now I'm moving into the operation_status logic.\n\nI'm making operation_status use a single query and pass the fetched operation directly into resolveIntent. For the copilot layer, I'm excluding copilot.brief events from agent activity and clarifying that fields like arguments/concept/summary inside system records are customer-provided text. On the UI side, I'm resetting manualIntentId on refresh if it's no longer among the dashboard's approvals. Finally I'm sketching out test cases: negation messages requiring a code, code-only confirmation completing, double expiry rejecting both operations, A/B proposal scenarios where A becomes unknown after a bank outage and B gets blocked, and concept-variant proposals being blocked while the prior one is still unknown.\n\nI'm also testing manually inserting an intent row in processing status so a new equivalent proposal gets blocked, verifying repeated proposals on the same intent reuse the same approvalId, and confirming operation_status resolves unknown with a single GET. On the client side, a 404 with a non-JSON body should still surface as 404. Ready to dispatch this.\n\nAmbas revisiones coinciden en los mismos vacíos: negación que cita el código, equivalencia estricta por JSON, `processing` no bloqueante, caducidad que se omite en el segundo intento, y pérdida del estado HTTP en cuerpos ilegibles. Con esto cierro el diseño para la segunda oleada.\n\n",
