      title:
        'Miguel wants to push forward with the P3 refactor, then video polish, then the session vi…',
      text: "Miguel wants to push forward with the P3 refactor, then video polish, then the session visualizer, all before Monday's deadline despite it being Saturday afternoon already. I think it's doable, but only with a disciplined sequence and gates: each step needs to leave the app behaviorally and visually identical, verified through tests, typecheck, build, and before/after screenshot comparisons.\n\nFor ordering, I'll tackle the API refactor first since it's smallest, then design tokens since they're low-risk, then the page.tsx split last since it's the riskiest. For the API layer, I'm keeping the Next.js catch-all route convention but organizing handlers into domain-specific modules with a shared context object and a pattern-based route table for matching paths, auth levels, and methods.\n\nFor the remaining domain files I need operator-facing and document-related handlers, and the catch-all route.ts just needs to parse the path, enforce same-origin and auth, find the matching route, and preserve existing error codes and ordering behavior. I'll verify with existing tests plus a new router unit test covering pattern matching and auth failures.\n\nFor the design tokens work, I'm planning to consolidate the ~25 stray hex colors in the 1387-line globals.css into named tokens along with spacing, radii, and type scales, then verify pixel-perfect visual parity with before/after Playwright screenshots across key pages, controlling for dynamic content like dates by using fixed DB state.\n\nNow for page.tsx, I'm mapping out how to break it into components under app/components/ — Sidebar, PersonSwitch, Icon, Notice, tab components, operator case views, and approval/pending panels — keeping state centralized in Home and passing it down as props, with actions either staying in Home or moving into a custom hook. I'm also considering splitting globals.css into per-component CSS files imported back into the main stylesheet.\n\nFor visual parity, I'm thinking I'll need to script a Playwright-based check that captures reference screenshots of the current state before refactoring, then verifies the refactored components match pixel-for-pixel. I need to check whether Playwright is available locally so I can write this as a standalone script, likely placed in a tools directory, and then break the migration into staged handoffs each gated by a parity check.\n\n",
