      seq: 3622,
      kind: 'thinking',
      title:
        "I'm refactoring the API routes, moving handlers to their respective files and defining ty…",
      text: "I'm refactoring the API routes, moving handlers to their respective files and defining types and dispatch logic in `router.ts`. The main challenge is preserving the original route matching order, especially for `conversations` and `actions`, and correctly handling the `session` route's public POST and authenticated GET methods. It seems the original logic for `session` might allow any method after authentication, which I need to clarify to ensure POST remains public and GET requires authentication.\n\nI'm re-evaluating the `conversations/:id` route. The original logic returns both the conversation and its messages for POST requests to `conversations/:id`, even though the explicit `/messages` subroute exists. This behavior needs to be preserved by ensuring the `conversations/:id` route (any method) is matched *after* `conversations/:id/messages` (POST). I also noticed a discrepancy in role checking: the root `conversations` route forbids customers, but `conversations/:id` relies on `conversationFor` which effectively filters by user ID, leading to a 404 for unauthorized users rather than a forbidden error. I'll preserve the original behavior where `conversations` is forbidden for customers, but `conversations/:id*` allows any authenticated user.\n\nI'm wrestling with the `actions` POST route's validation and authorization order. The draft spec implies Zod parsing should happen *before* the role check, meaning an invalid request body would result in a 400 (bad request) even for an operator, not a 403 (forbidden). However, the lead's test case expects a 403 for Marta (an operator) with a valid body. To preserve the original behavior precisely, especially for invalid bodies, I'll set the route's auth to 'any' and perform the role check manually within the handler after Zod parsing.\n\nI'm noticing several routes have subtle differences in when role checks occur relative to parsing. For `preview-answer` and `ingestion`, the role check happens before parsing, unlike `search` which only parses. `incidents` checks roles upfront, making all `incidents/*` routes operator-only, including GET requests. The `approvals/:id/confirm` route originally had no explicit role check, allowing operators to confirm their own (non-existent) approvals, resulting in a 404 for others; I'll preserve this with 'any' auth. `documents` also has no role check, relying on visible filtering, and uses 'any' auth. GET requests to `approvals/x/confirm` currently result in a 404 because my pattern matching is POST-only.\n\nI'm refining the route matching for `conversations/:id/messages`. The original logic returns conversation and messages for any method on this path, not just POST. To preserve this, I'll define an explicit `ANY` handler for `conversations/:id/messages` that mirrors the `conversations/:id` detail handler, ensuring it's ordered after the specific POST route but before the general `:id` route. This maintains the original behavior where non-POST requests to `/messages` still return conversation details.\n\nI'm mapping error codes to HTTP statuses: MissingOpenAIKeyError gets 503, HttpError/BankError get their own status, ZodError/SyntaxError get 400, and everything else defaults to 500 with a console error. I'll place the `sameOrigin` check for POST requests at the beginning of the dispatch logic. The session token will be set via a `Set-Cookie` header in the session POST handler.",
