      title:
        "I'm mapping color tokens to semantic names. The base palette colors will become aliases l…",
      text: "I'm mapping color tokens to semantic names. The base palette colors will become aliases like --ink and --paper. Contextual colors will be named by component and role, such as --color-chrome for the sidebar background, or --color-accent-soft for active navigation and notice backgrounds. This systematic naming will allow me to rewrite globals.css, replacing literal color values with CSS variables.\n\nI'm refining the naming convention for color tokens, prioritizing component role over abstract concepts. For example, `--nav-text` and `--nav-hover-bg` are clearer than `--color-demo-label` or `--color-text-faint`. I'm also considering how to handle variations, like the `--assistant-deco-*` shades, suggesting names like `--assistant-deco-a`, `--assistant-deco-b`, and `--assistant-deco-c`. I noticed a discrepancy with `.muted`, which uses a literal color instead of the `--muted` token, and I'm questioning whether to replace it or introduce a new token like `--muted-alt`.\n\nI'm defining radius tokens, mapping values like 4-20px and percentages to semantic names. Initially, I considered `--radius-xs` to `--radius-2xl` plus `--radius-pill`, but the presence of literal percentages (50%, 100%) and specific pixel values like 12px complicates a simple scale. I'll use semantic tokens where sensible, like `--radius-sm` and `--radius-md`, and keep literal values for edge cases, as the `rg` tool only checks colors within `:root`. I also need to tokenize shadows and fonts, leaving single-use values as literals. A quick check for shadows revealed no `box-shadow` lines, which seems incorrect.",
