        'Splitting the media queries per component file seems to change their relative order, pote…',
      text: "Splitting the media queries per component file seems to change their relative order, potentially causing conflicts. While same-specificity conflicts within a file are preserved, cross-file conflicts are unlikely due to distinct selectors. The original order mixed domains, but the new order follows file order.\n\nMoving operator media queries to the end of operator.css could break specificity. In the original file, reconciliation rules followed the case-detail media queries, giving reconciliation priority. If the media queries move to the end, they might override reconciliation rules, though the selectors are currently disjoint, which should keep things safe.\n\nKeeping the four media blocks together in `responsive.css` and importing it last seems the most robust approach, preserving their internal order. Moving non-media selectors like `.reconciliation` and `.brief` before the media queries in `operator.css` is unlikely to cause conflicts, as their selectors are distinct from the media selectors. Creating a separate `operator-late.css` would be overkill. I'll keep them at the end of `operator.css` and import `responsive.css` last, but I'll verify parity at 1440px anyway, even though none of the media queries apply at that resolution.\n\nI'm deciding where to place the media queries. Since they don't apply at the capture size, I can group them at the end of `responsive.css`. Alternatively, I could append them to `base.css`, but `responsive.css` seems cleaner. I'll proceed with `responsive.css` and note this addition. Next, I'll focus on the component code, defining types and functions for API calls, formatting, and creating the `Icon` and `useBananaApp` components. I'll then detail the props and callbacks for each presentational component: `Sidebar`, `TopBar`, `Notice`, `OverviewTab`, `ChatTab`, `TransferTab`, and `DocumentsTab`.\n\nI'm organizing the UI components, defining their props and callbacks. `ApprovalsPanel` needs `approvals`, `accountLabel`, `contactLabel`, and `onConfirm`. `PendingOperationsPanel` requires `ops`, `contactLabel`, and `onRefresh`. The `operator/CasesTab` will manage `current name`, `dashboard.incidents`, `people`, `selectedCase`, `brief`, `busy`, and callbacks for `openCase`, `closeCase`, `reconcile`, and `draftBrief`. Similarly, `operator/CaseList` needs `incidents`, `people`, `selectedId`, and `onOpen`, while `operator/CaseDetail` handles `selectedCase`, `brief`, `busy`, `onClose`, `onReconcile`, and `onDraft`. `operator/CopilotBrief` takes `brief`. The `page.tsx` hook, `useBananaApp`, will manage messages, scrolling effects, and return a large object, including `accountLabel` and `contactLabel` which depend on the dashboard state. I'll ensure the JSX remains identical.",
