      title:
        'Wait — the order of rules matters for the cascade. My file split must preserve the origin…',
      text: "Wait — the order of rules matters for the cascade. My file split must preserve the original sequence. If I move media queries to a separate file imported later, their rules might override component styles that should come first. Keeping media queries within their component files could also alter cascade order. The safest approach seems to be creating ordered files: tokens, base, then component files in their original relative order.\n\nI'm considering how to split the CSS files while preserving the cascade order. Shared selectors like `.text-button` appear interleaved with component-specific rules. Moving them to an earlier file like `base.css` risks changing their specificity relative to later rules. To be safe, I'll maintain the original order by slicing files into contiguous line ranges, accepting that shared selectors will reside in the first file that uses them.\n\nI'm considering how to handle the footer, which spans across multiple files. While placing it in `overview.css` is semantically impure, it's order-safe. Moving it to `base.css` is semantically cleaner and safe due to no competing rules. I'll partition semantically and verify pixel parity, as the comparator will catch any rendering changes. Media queries will be distributed into component files, moving earlier in the cascade, which is acceptable if parity holds. The simplest structure is `tokens.css` for :root.\n\nI'm reconsidering the file split strategy. While contiguous slices are simpler, the spec says shared selectors *should* go to `base.css`. I'll move them there, preserving their relative order, even if it means taking them from later files like `overview.css`. This seems the most pragmatic approach to satisfy both the order requirement and the spec.\n\nI'm moving shared selectors like `.case-empty` and `.subtle-tag` to `base.css`, as they're used across multiple components. I've also confirmed that moving `.primary-button` to `base.css` won't cause issues, as later rules in `overview.css` like `.support-card .text-button` and `.transfer-form .primary-button` will still correctly override it due to their higher specificity or later position. The `.demo-label` duplication between `sidebar.css` and `topbar.css` is handled by keeping the earlier rule in `sidebar.css` and the later one in `topbar.css`, preserving cascade order.\n\nI'm consolidating the footer, alerts, spinner, and keyframes into `base.css`, placing them after the shared rules. Then, I'll slice the remaining CSS files (`chat.css`, `transfer.css`, `panels.css`, `operator.css`, `documents.css`) into contiguous line ranges, ensuring shared selectors like `.approvals` and `.muted` are handled correctly by either placing them in `base.css` or within their first component file.\n\nI'm reconsidering the placement of media queries. Initially, I thought about a separate `responsive.css` file, but the original file order suggests they should be distributed within component files, appended at the end of each. This approach preserves the cascade order better, especially since component files like `.reconciliation` lack their own media rules. I'll extract the media query lines to examine their contents and decide on the best distribution strategy.",
