        'The CSS split and reordering seem to be the cause of the rendering difference, not compon…',
      text: "The CSS split and reordering seem to be the cause of the rendering difference, not component changes. I'm investigating which specific rule reordering is responsible for the +2px shift. Conflicts only arise when rules with identical specificity target the same property on the same element. I'm checking for duplicate selectors and their order, like `.demo-label` and `.message-avatar`, and also examining cases where a rule moved earlier might now precede another, such as `.subtle-tag` and `.chat-header .subtle-tag`.\n\nI'm exploring two potential causes for the +2px page height increase. First, the `.app-shell`'s `min-height: 100vh` might be causing content to grow, affecting the main element's calculated height. Alternatively, a change in `main`'s height, possibly due to `.page-heading`'s margin collapsing differently, could be the culprit. Animation rules like `@keyframes spin` and `pulse` are unlikely to be the cause. I'm now focusing on property conflicts between rules of identical specificity whose relative order has changed due to my base hoisting.\n\nI'm re-evaluating specificity conflicts. While `.nav-item` and `.primary-button` have equal specificity, the original order favored `.nav-item`. Now, hoisted rules place `.primary-button` earlier, potentially causing issues. I'm also checking `p` tag styling and `button` vs. `.primary-button` order, but the main conflict seems to be `.support-card .text-button` versus `.text-button`, where the descendant selector originally had higher precedence. I'll use a binary search approach to pinpoint the exact rule causing the shift.\n\nThe text shift seems to be a letter-spacing issue, not a positional one. The `.eyebrow` class might have its `letter-spacing: 1.8px` overridden, possibly because the same class is used elsewhere, like in `CaseDetail`. I'm going to bisect the CSS build to isolate the exact rule causing this.\n\nWait, the media queries might be the issue. They were previously between the `.documents.css` and `.reconciliation-row` rules, but now `responsive.css` loads last, after `.reconciliation-row`'s file. This means the media queries aren't applying correctly at 1440px. Also, `@keyframes spin` moved into `base.css`, which is irrelevant to keyframe order. I'll bisect by putting hoisted rules back inline to test this.\n\nThe contiguous split test is unnecessary because the earlier test already confirmed CSS splitting, not contiguousness, is the cause. I'll skip that and bisect the hoisted set to find the conflicting pair. The shift isn't from `font` shorthand, and prettier didn't reformat the CSS. The only difference is rule order, so I'll find all duplicate selectors like `.demo-label` and `.message-avatar` where order matters.",
