      title:
        'The rule reordering is causing a +2px shift, breaking parity. I need to bisect to find th…',
      text: "The rule reordering is causing a +2px shift, breaking parity. I need to bisect to find the exact conflicting pair. It seems the hoisted rules, like `.page-heading` and `.text-button`, are being placed before rules that originally preceded them, but I'm not seeing direct conflicts with the sidebar/topbar rules. The issue might be subtler, possibly with `.subtle-tag` and `.status-pill`, though they target different elements.\n\nI'm re-evaluating the rule order, specifically how hoisted rules like `.muted` and `.case-empty` interact with others. It seems the original order had media queries before reconciliation rules, but my current ordering flips this by placing reconciliation rules (within operator.css) before media queries (in responsive.css). This flip only matters at smaller screen sizes, but I'm still investigating which specific rule pair causes the pixel shift, focusing on elements matched by both hoisted and sidebar/topbar rules.\n\nI'm analyzing the CSS rules for the sidebar and topbar sections (192-398). It seems there are no direct overlaps in specificity between elements like `.breadcrumb span` and `.footer span`, or between `main` and `.page-heading` descendants, even though they might share class selectors. The overview remainder (415-653) shows `.support-card .text-button` correctly outranking a standalone `.text-button`. For the chat section (719-909), `.chat-header .subtle-tag` also has higher specificity than `.subtle-tag` alone, so its placement doesn't seem to be the issue. Finally, in the transfer section (911-975), a `label` element selector doesn't appear to conflict with hoisted rules.\n\nI'm struggling with static analysis, so I'll use empirical bisecting with real captures. The hoisted groups are {A: 399-414, B:513-525, C:531-551, D:608-632, E:654-718, F:760-766, G:1072-1093}. I'll test with half hoisted, but need a faster single-shot capture; a parity script captures 8 elements, which is fine. Alternatively, the issue might be that the tail rules are dominant, or that `@import` requests failed partially in dev, causing missing base rules.\n\nI'm reconsidering the +2px shift: it might not be CSS-related. A functional flow created and then deleted an intent/approval, which the normalizer removed. This happened after captures were taken, so the transfer capture shows a filled but unsaved form, and the chat capture shows unchanged messages. The +2px could be a scrollbar appearing due to a full-page layout with scrolling. Empirically, if splitting files is neutral, reordering must be the cause, so I'll bisect to confirm.",
