      title:
        "I've got the formats settled enough, so now I'll wait for Cursor's third review before di…",
      text: "I've got the formats settled enough, so now I'll wait for Cursor's third review before dispatching the visualizer build. I'm settling on the design: an HTML/JS/CSS viewer with a D3 vendor bundle and per-session data files, generated by a build script.\n\nI'm sketching out a normalized data model where each session has nodes (messages, thinking, tool calls/results) linked by parent IDs into lanes, plus stats like message counts and durations. Then I'm working out how to map each agent's raw logs into this model: Devin's node/chain structure becomes lanes and tool-call/result pairs, while Codex's rollout events get parsed by type to reconstruct the same parent-child structure.\n\nFor the UI, I'm planning a header with agent filters and search, a left sidebar listing sessions grouped by agent plus a global timeline so reviewers can see when sessions ran relative to each other, and a main swimlane view where each session's nodes are laid out left-to-right by sequence, color-coded by kind, with curved parent-child edges showing branches, zoomable via d3 and clickable for detail.\n\nClicking a node opens a detail panel with full text, pretty-printed tool args, timestamps, and token counts, with keyboard navigation and filters for hiding tool results or thinking; search highlights matching nodes, and I'm considering Spanish labels since judges are in Spain. I also want a \"Resumen\" tab per session that shows just the human's turns as chapter markers for quick navigation, styled with the Banana palette, with performance tuned for roughly 3500 SVG nodes.\n\nFor acceptance testing, I should verify it opens correctly via file:// in Playwright, take a screenshot, confirm node counts match the manifest, and ensure there are no network requests. Then there needs to be a final re-export and rebuild step for Miguel, documented in the README since the build must be re-run after the final export.\n\n",
