      kind: 'thinking',
      title:
        "I'm structuring the build process, creating a generator for session viewers and defining …",
      text: "I'm structuring the build process, creating a generator for session viewers and defining the viewer's source files. The generator will read session manifests, normalize data for each session, and write it into `data/<agent>-<id>.js` files. The viewer's `index.html` will dynamically load these session files. I need to carefully handle role assignment for reviewers, sorting them by start time to determine their round number.\n\nI'm refining the node ID strategy to ensure uniqueness across messages and tool calls, considering parent relationships for tool results. Assistant messages with both content and tool calls will now emit separate 'assistant' and 'thinking' nodes when both are present, with 'thinking' preceding 'assistant'. I'll also count image occurrences by checking for 'image' in extensions.\n\nI'm mapping the message structure, distinguishing between system, user, and assistant roles. Reasoning will be tagged as 'thinking', and tool calls will be structured with their name and arguments. Tool outputs will be linked to their parent calls. I'll skip recording world states and token usage, focusing instead on the core message flow and reasoning representation.\n\nI'm trying to reconcile source message counts with normalized node counts. The source counts user, assistant, and tool messages, while the normalized counts break down assistant messages further into 'assistant', 'thinking', 'tool_call', and 'tool_result' nodes. My initial check, `normalized(user+assistant+tool_call+tool_result) >=? user+assistant(source)`, seems flawed. A key issue is how to count assistant messages that have tool calls but no explicit content or thinking; these might inflate the source count relative to the normalized count. A simpler check comparing source counts (user+assistant+tool) against normalized counts (user+assistant+tool_call+tool_result+thinking) also fails because tool calls are split.\n\nI'm refining the check for message role counts. The normalized count includes user, assistant, tool_result, thinking, and tool_call nodes, while the source count uses user, assistant, and tool. Assistant messages with tool calls but no content or thinking need careful handling to avoid miscounting. I'll ensure an 'assistant' node is emitted even with empty content if it contains tool calls, to keep counts consistent. This logic will be generalized for other agents like codex/cursor.\n\nI'm structuring the visualization: lanes are rows with fixed height, and node positions are calculated based on sequence number. Links connect nodes horizontally, skipping hidden ones by finding the nearest visible ancestor. User nodes in the main lane form chapters with labels. Filters use checkboxes, defaulting to 'thinking' and 'tool_result' visible, with 'system' hidden. A detail panel shows selected node info and navigation buttons. Search highlights matching nodes. A global counter tracks sessions, nodes, tool calls, and duration. The left panel displays timeline bars for sessions, colored by agent, with a grouped session list below. Hash links navigate to specific sessions and nodes. Performance is good with SVG circles for ~3500 nodes, but labels are only in tooltips.\n\nI'm refining the node ID strategy to ensure uniqueness and maintain parent linkage. For Devin messages, I'll use string IDs like `d{node_id}` for messages and `d{node_id}t{k}` for tool calls, while codex/cursor will use `c{i}`/`u{i}`. Internal nodes will get sequential IDs `m{seq}`, storing the original `node_id` in `src`. Parent linkage will be managed by tracking the last emitted node for each raw node ID. For tool results, the parent will be the tool call node or the last emitted node for the parent message. For other node types, the parent will be the last emitted node for the parent message, or null if none exists. Lane management will distinguish between Devin's 'chain' and codex/cursor's 'main' lanes. For nodes with null parents, I'll infer the parent lane by scanning for the first node that resolves to another lane.\n\nI'm trying to resolve parent-child relationships for nodes across different branches, specifically noting that node 169's parent is in branch 170, but both seem to belong to branch 170. It's unclear if node 169 belongs to the 'sidekick' lane, and its ultimate parent is still unknown, relying on the generator for resolution. I'm also establishing lane ordering based on the sequence of the first node in each lane, with approximately 330 lanes, and defining label mappings: 'main' to 'principal', 'sidekick' to 'subagente sidekick', and 'branch@N' to 'rama @N', while retaining the raw label.",
