        'The graph is actually unreadable at this scale — 262 lanes makes it roughly 8k pixels tal…',
      text: "The graph is actually unreadable at this scale — 262 lanes makes it roughly 8k pixels tall and 100k pixels wide, so the \"one lane per branch\" approach completely breaks down here. I need to rethink the layout, especially since Devin's branch chains seem to number in the hundreds, likely because each tool-call turn or message regeneration spawns its own branch in the internal tree.\n\nLooking closer, most branches are tiny (a handful of nodes) while a few dominate with hundreds of nodes, so I should only give dedicated lanes to chains above some size threshold and draw small branches as short inline stubs beneath their parent. The x-axis is also way too wide if spread across all 3483 nodes by sequence, so I'm considering a compact per-lane positioning scheme with horizontal scroll and a minimap, or better yet, collapsing consecutive tool call/result pairs into a single expandable turn-level node — that feels like the right default view, with expansion revealing the full detail on click.\n\nI should add a full-width minimap strip at the bottom showing viewport navigation, start the initial view at zoom 1 on the first user node instead of fitting the whole sliver, and default-filter out thinking/tool_result nodes to cut clutter. Chapters should get fixed-position header labels above the zoomed SVG that are clickable and deduped. I'll have the sidekick implement these layout changes and re-verify with zoom-1 screenshots, plus add tooltips for extra context.\n\nEl visor funciona pero el grafo queda ilegible: una calle por cada una de las 262 ramas con paso fijo lo aplana al ajustar a pantalla. Voy a rediseñar el layout usando calles solo para cadenas grandes, ramas pequeñas como ramitas junto a su bifurcación, eje x compacto por calle, y minimapa con capítulos fijos fuera del zoom.\n\n",
