        "Okay, I've mapped out the viewer rework. Big lanes get their own rows, small ones get inl…",
      text: "Okay, I've mapped out the viewer rework. Big lanes get their own rows, small ones get inlined twigs with stacking and aggregation. Compact X uses local indexing per street, starting from the fork node's X for streets with rows. The initial view centers on the first main user node at zoom 1, with new \"Ajustar\" and \"Zoom 1\" buttons. A minimap sits below the graph, and chapters above, with tooltips and node styling for calls and results. I need to ensure the red border on tool calls correctly maps to their failed results.\n\nI'm refining the layout logic. For big lanes, nodes are indexed sequentially within the lane, with X positions calculated based on the lane's start and local index. Sidekick lanes and big branches will use the X position of their fork parent node. Overlapping X ranges for main lane nodes before a fork are acceptable as streets are rows. Twig links will be anchored at the fork node's position, even if the node itself is hidden, and drawn dashed to the first twig node. This layout needs to recompute whenever the filter state changes because node visibility and indexing are affected.\n\nI'm drawing only visible Twig nodes, calculating their X and Y positions based on the anchor, lane, and stack level. To manage overlapping X ranges within the same lane and level, I'll increment the stack level. If a twig exceeds three levels, I'll group the remaining lanes into an aggregate \"n ramas\" node at the anchor's position, displaying the lane count in a tooltip. This aggregate node will be rendered as a circle with hatching and a count, using the same HTML tooltip mechanism as other nodes. Its kind will be neutral, represented by a `.cluster` element that isn't selectable or searchable. Node positions will be recomputed on every render or filter change, and the local index for node X calculation will only consider visible nodes. Node order within a lane will remain sequential.\n\nI'm refining the layout for visible nodes: big lanes get row assignments, while others are positioned relative to their fork parent's X coordinate, with a fallback to the nearest visible ancestor's X if the fork node is hidden. For the minimap, I'll use a canvas element to render lane lines and scaled graph elements, updating it after layout changes; clicking it will adjust the main view's transform. Pan performance is good, relying solely on transform attribute changes. For chapter labels, I'll recompute their X positions based on the transform and hide overlapping ones, prioritizing those closest to the viewport center. Tooltips will be HTML divs triggered by mouse events on node groups.\n\nI'm marking failed tool calls with a red border by adding a `result-false` class to the tool call node if its corresponding `tool_result` has `success===false`. For zoom controls, I'll add a \"Zoom 1\" button to reset the view to scale 1, centered on the current view. The initial view will center the first main user node at zoom 1 unless a specific node is provided via hash. For drawing links, `visParent` should naturally handle most cases, including links from fork nodes to the first visible node in a lane, so I'll drop the special `forkLinks` logic but keep the `fork` class for cross-lane links.\n\nI'm refining the twig stacking logic: for hidden fork nodes, I'll compute a virtual position or anchor to the nearest visible ancestor, aligning with the \"jump to next visible\" behavior. For aggregation, I'll group remaining lanes into a single cluster node after three levels, positioned at the next available slot or unplaced anchor, with a tooltip listing the branches. I'm also implementing FPS measurement via programmatic panning and zoom within rAF, and planning a substantial rewrite of viewer.js along with CSS and HTML additions for chapters, minimap, and tooltips. Default filters are being updated, and I'll proceed with writing the new viewer.js.",
